{
  "slug": "verbless-clause",
  "title": "Verbless Clause",
  "definition": "A clause in which the [term:predicate] is a noun phrase, adjective, or prepositional phrase with no overt verb. The [term:copula] is absent. Verbless clauses are the most common strategy for [term:classification-predicate]s and [term:equative-predicate]s cross-linguistically, realized through juxtaposition (bare subject + predicate NP) or predicative marking (a suffix on the predicate NP). In many languages like Russian, the verbless clause is restricted to the present tense, with a copula appearing in past and future. In languages like Hamer, a dedicated predicate marker -ne signals the predicate NP. In languages like Nenets (Dixon §3.5), what appears to be a verbless clause is instead a [term:denominal-strategy]: the noun takes verbal agreement affixes.",
  "relatedTerms": ["predicate", "copula", "subject", "classification-predicate", "equative-predicate", "denominal-strategy"],
